jros2: recycle the shared-memory buffer handles instead of allocating one per message

SharedMemManager::Segment::alloc_buffer() (every message or fragment sent through the SHM transport) and
SharedMemManager::Listener::pop() (every one received) create a std::shared_ptr<SharedMemBuffer> with make_shared: one
operator new and one free per message on the sending thread and on the receiving thread (see the "TODO(Adolfo):
Dynamic allocation" notes). The handles are now created with allocate_shared and an allocator that keeps released
blocks for reuse in one lock-free stack (compare-and-swap on a version-tagged index), shared by every thread: no lock
between a realtime publishing thread and the transport threads, and no per-thread state, so a thread that sends or
receives through shared memory for the first time (e.g. the event thread's first heartbeat over SHM, long after start)
does not allocate either. A new chunk of 64 blocks is carved from the heap, under a mutex, only when no block is free:
for the first handle of the process, and when more handles than ever before are alive at once.

